      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50020072,
          "author": "theodorejb",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-09T13:16:33.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50020072",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; We will support the Deno runtime for another year with monthly releases containing bug fixes and security updates. After that year we will end our development of the Deno runtime. Deno will remain open source, and we welcome others who want to continue its development.<p>So unless someone else picks up development, Deno will no longer be supported.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50020218,
          "author": "binlog",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-09T13:26:19.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50020218",
          "textZhHtml": "This is a wild detail to just bury at the bottom. So many companies went all-in on Deno in recent years. Some even did major migrations off Node.js. Sucks for them I guess, but that's always the risk in chasing the shiny new thing over sticking to the old and dependable."
        },
        {
          "id": 50028237,
          "author": "coke12",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T00:27:50.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50028237",
          "textZhHtml": "If it was truly “so many companies” then they wouldn’t be in this situation… reading between the lines here it’s clear Deno was not a successful project."
        },
        {
          "id": 50028692,
          "author": "oersted",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T01:46:04.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50028692",
          "textZhHtml": "It might not have been a successful project as a VC growth startup, but lots of people saw that coming here in HN when they started raising millions.<p>In the space of such foundational technologies, there’s always been a vast gap between the number of private vs open-source projects that succeed. Even all the way back to proprietary compilers 40-50 years ago.</p><p>If they had stuck to being an open-source project, the kind of adoption and mindshare they achieved would have put them among the most successful ever. And I am sure they would have been able get their team paid very well sustainably. The unicorn model cannot fit every single venture.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50029667,
          "author": "pjmlp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T04:47:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50029667",
          "textZhHtml": "If they cannot pay bills, because no one pays upstream, then it is a failure as company, regardless of VC growth.<p>Some day devs will learn FOSS projects don't survive on good will alone.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50029912,
          "author": "oersted",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T05:36:42.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50029912",
          "textZhHtml": "An open-source project with their success could have easily paid their bills through sponsorships and support contracts, as many others do.<p>But the VC path pushed them to increase costs more than they should. They didn’t need that money to deliver on the mandate of Deno, they had to inflate the vision to some grand cloud solution, which didn’t make much sense.</p><p>&gt; then it is a failure as company</p><p>Perhaps it shouldn’t be a company at all. Private companies cannot attract the same support as open-source foundations do, since they are for-profit organizations.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50029948,
          "author": "pjmlp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T05:42:01.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50029948",
          "textZhHtml": "No they don't, they survive like street performers, some days the hat is full enough for a warm meal.<p>Foundations are companies in disguise, with taxes benefits, and there isn't one without industry partners.</p><p>Which programming language survives with support contracts, no company sponsorship, and has mainstream adoption?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50032613,
          "author": "sailfast",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T13:13:46.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50032613",
          "textZhHtml": "Was going to suggest Python but that doesn’t pass your “foundations don’t count” logic (that I don’t really agree with)<p>Of COURSE there are industry partners. That’s not a bad thing!</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50032559,
          "author": "sokoloff",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T13:06:27.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50032559",
          "textZhHtml": "C? c++?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50035683,
          "author": "pseudosavant",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T18:26:31.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50035683",
          "textZhHtml": "It was successful for the Node community, but not for Deno the runtime or business. Deno launched with many features Node didn't have, but that it has now adopted: ES Modules, fetch(), .ts file support, web streams, web crypto, a built-in test runner, file watching, etc. Node's APIs didn't even use Promises/async/await, they were all callback-based.<p>Ultimately, it was easier for the Node ecosystem to adopt those changes into Node than it was to migrate to a different JS runtime like Deno. Bun would likely not exist without Deno too.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50041064,
          "author": "2color",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T08:53:47.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50041064",
          "textZhHtml": "A bit like how ES2015 adopted a lot of features from CoffeeScript"
        },
        {
          "id": 50042301,
          "author": "jchook",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T11:57:09.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50042301",
          "textZhHtml": "Sadly they forgot comprehensions, one of the most elegant features of CoffeeScript."
        },
        {
          "id": 50046921,
          "author": "mlhaufe",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T19:29:11.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50046921",
          "textZhHtml": "The didn't forget. JavaScript had them as a Firefox extension but dropped them due to incompatibility in the standard:<p><a href=\"https://esdiscuss.org/topic/a-reprieve-for-es6-comprehensions\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://esdiscuss.org/topic/a-reprieve-for-es6-comprehension...</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50028585,
          "author": "makeitdouble",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T01:27:38.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50028585",
          "textZhHtml": "Being successful or not depends on the scale of the company. I'd wager at Cloudflare scale, many otherwise decent businesses would have virtually no effect on their baseline and wouldn't be worth running."
        },
        {
          "id": 50029990,
          "author": "pjmlp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T05:49:14.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50029990",
          "textZhHtml": "Regardless of the size, being successful means, is there enough money in the bank at the end of each month."
        },
        {
          "id": 50030136,
          "author": "makeitdouble",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T06:12:48.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50030136",
          "textZhHtml": "You're defining being sustainable. IMHO \"successful\" is a lot more relative and defined by expectations.<p>To jest, businesses can be successful without sustainability. Instagram definitely was.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50030454,
          "author": "pjmlp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T07:06:48.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50030454",
          "textZhHtml": "If being successful means pumping an idea for acquisition."
        },
        {
          "id": 50031284,
          "author": "wongarsu",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T09:54:23.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50031284",
          "textZhHtml": "Or if being successful means building something billions of people use on a regular basis"
        },
        {
          "id": 50033964,
          "author": "lstodd",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T15:36:05.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50033964",
          "textZhHtml": "like, idk, nginx?"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50019911.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-10-13-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50047422,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50047422",
      "rank": 3,
      "title": "The gap in shared understanding of LLM capability is widening",
      "titleZh": "The gap in shared understanding of LLM capability is widening",
      "url": "https://twitter.com/karpathy/status/2109361546505966046",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50047422",
      "author": "albertzeyer",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T20:20:52.000Z",
      "score": 694,
      "commentsCount": 1015,
      "bestRank": 3,
      "bestRankSource": "beststories",
      "maxScore": 694,
      "maxComments": 1015,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-12T19:18:04.300Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-10-13T08:27:33.913Z",
      "appearances": 6,
      "category": "AI",
      "summaryZh": [
        "Dusting off this tweet from April because this gap in shared understanding of LLM capability is *widening*. It's now less \"two groups of people speaking past each other\" and more a sharp funnel.",
        "- Napkin math somewhere around 6B people (~75% of the population) have barely come in contact with LLMs at all. - Around 1-2B (~20%) are casual and infrequent users of free-tier ChatGPT-like products. This group sees derpy chatbots and treats them a bit like a better Google search, a writing aid, or etc. Maybe an agent tries to book you a flight. I have non-tech friends who (reasonably, imo) say they have not much use for it at all. Even many professionals outside of math&code are in this tier. For example, execs and many other functions spend a lot of their time talking to other people, so while they understand what is happening intellectually, it is still second-hand and a bit abstract. - Now we get to professional use of frontier-grade LLMs in math&code. Somewhere around 20M people (0.2%) see first-hand that large, complex projects that used to take them weeks/months can now be completed by agents with a prompt. Building apps, copying apps, translating apps, decompiling apps from binaries... This has all happened very quickly and recently - less than 1 year ago, I was writing code manually by hand, typing memorized computer code commands into a code editor character by character, occasionally pressing Tab to autocomplete a little chunk of code. - And finally we get to the ~5,000 people (~0.00006%) with access to frontier-grade systems internally. The external world has seen the preview. It looks like swarms of thousands of agents collaborating over weeks on software mega projects: minting zero days, running cyber attacks and defenses at machine speeds, discovering new science, advancing the frontier of mathematics. Things that would have taken top professionals in the industry years of work. Meanwhile, human review and comprehension are starting to fall behind. For example, people are still involved in the \"archeology\" of the OpenAI-HF incident from many months ago. Mathematicians may be poring over the 722 manuscripts on frontier mathematics for a while."
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50048335,
          "author": "frereubu",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T22:02:22.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50048335",
          "textZhHtml": "<a href=\"https://xxcancel.com/karpathy/status/2109361546505966046\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://xxcancel.com/karpathy/status/2109361546505966046</a>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50048499,
          "author": "flecomet",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T22:19:44.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50048499",
          "textZhHtml": "you are my hero"
        },
        {
          "id": 50049182,
          "author": "usernomdeguerre",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T23:27:44.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049182",
          "textZhHtml": "I feel like if the gap were so monumental that he could just point to a monument of it, rather than hand-waving at \"the vertical professionals\".<p>Alternative interpretation of the numbers; there's only a subset of productive work that LLM's are 'capable' of doing at this point. That is, work that's verifiable by a machine or doesn't require verification. Thus, the cleavage points are along that axis and-- naturally-- most human beings don't have that kind of work.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50053849,
          "author": "pjc50",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T09:45:33.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50053849",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; That is, work that's verifiable by a machine or doesn't require verification<p>That's where I'm at on the capabilities. LLMs have demolished software and mathematics because those have well-developed verification that's easier than doing the thing itself. Stuff that's more subjective they <i>can</i> produce good work, but with a significant error rate which makes it hard to let them loose on important client work without checking. Or rather, a number of companies are going through the cycle of trying that, having an expensive error, and reconsidering.</p><p>Or sometimes a company can simply pass the error cost onto consumers, who are gradually arming themselves with LLMs in response. We're going to see a productivity-sapping boom in disputes.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50054754,
          "author": "omnicognate",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T11:22:44.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054754",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; demolished software and mathematics because those have well-developed verification that's easier than doing the thing itself<p>They are proving useful for the parts of those things that have that verification, but in neither case is that the whole of the field. In maths, for example, we have seen LLMs find answers to some interesting problems/conjectures, but haven't yet seen them pose any similarly interesting problems/conjectures themselves because the criteria for a \"good conjecture\" are unclear whereas the criteria for a resolution of an existing conjecture are usually supplied by the conjecture itself.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50068010,
          "author": "d2ou",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T07:25:42.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50068010",
          "textZhHtml": "See the excellent \"LLM can't jump\" position paper on this : with the main illustration being the discovery of general relativity.<p><a href=\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=klU4737opt\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://openreview.net/forum?id=klU4737opt</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50057871,
          "author": "ndriscoll",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T15:30:30.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057871",
          "textZhHtml": "Did anyone ask? i.e. did anyone with enough background to be able to judge whether a conjecture is \"good\" just point it at the repo of solved problems and ask it to formulate new conjectures based on the results/proofs? Or ask it to propose new structures to study? Or to identify a previously unknown general technique/tool that it had used in a proof?<p>It was asked to do a specific thing, and then its brain turned off/was reallocated to something else entirely (e.g. CRUD programming or answering celebrity trivia). It doesn't just idly think in its downtime. If we want it to do something else, we need to ask.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50060484,
          "author": "simoncion",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T18:16:04.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50060484",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Did anyone ask?<p>Given how... <i>motivated</i> the major LLM manufacturers are for good press? I've <i>zero</i> doubt that they have.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50064774,
          "author": "casey2",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T00:02:07.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064774",
          "textZhHtml": "This is just moving the goalposts, yet again, all of mathematics has been hyper optimized on solving problem, proving theorems getting results and writing papers.<p>Only now that their job (the elites) has been automated was it really about explain results and theory building all this, despite those actions getting explicitly rejected from all the top journals (sans maybe math journals intended for a undergrad/high-school readership). People who weren't in the club and genuinely enjoy math are having the time of their life proving results. There is just a lot of groupthink and silent majority effects due to said elites running departments.</p><p>I use \"theory-building\" separately to Gowers since what he's talking about is field creation that solves tons of problems at once (Grothendieck, FTC, etc (I'll add AI itself here as a meta theory)) not making connections after the fact for the benefit of the lesser humans' understanding.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50064874,
          "author": "dieortin",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T00:14:15.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064874",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Only now that their job (the elites) has been automated<p>I’m sorry, are you seriously referring to mathematicians as elites?</p><p>&gt; People who weren't in the club and genuinely enjoy math are having the time of their life proving results</p><p>If they couldn’t do so before and they can now, it sounds like they don’t understand what their LLMs are producing anyways. That doesn’t sound very fun to me. If they genuinely enjoyed math, maybe they would want to understand it instead of prompting an LLM to do stuff for them?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50066247,
          "author": "mslt",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T03:27:22.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50066247",
          "textZhHtml": "I’m also guessing that “in the club” from this person’s perspective is “studied mathematics long enough to have an academic career in the field,” and the other group that “genuinely enjoy math” are people who didn’t put in said work but can now watch in amazement as they prompt their way through something they fundamentally don’t understand."
        },
        {
          "id": 50065294,
          "author": "omnicognate",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T01:10:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065294",
          "textZhHtml": "I have literally no idea what you're on about. All I said was we haven't seen LLMs produce any new conjectures/problems of a similar level to the ones they've been solving (and I even said \"yet\"). Have you seen any? What does this rant about \"elites\" have to do with my comment?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50054054,
          "author": "bvcp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T10:05:23.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054054",
          "textZhHtml": "lowering the cost and speeding up delivery will mature technology in other fields leading go the disruptive verification that ai needs"
        },
        {
          "id": 50049543,
          "author": "mizzao",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T00:08:51.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049543",
          "textZhHtml": "The swarm-based HF hack, and the observation that 40-50% of the compute at AI labs goes into training the next generation of agents, doesn't count?<p>We're all over here using agents to code projects that used to take weeks and feeling pretty good about it, and they're using agents to build the next generation of frontier models — an order of magnitude more complex problem...</p><p>Whenever someone finds out how to apply this level of agentic sophistication to a problem that actually matters to consumers, then you'll have that monument that everyone can see.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50053751,
          "author": "pjc50",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T09:36:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50053751",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Whenever someone finds out how to apply this level of agentic sophistication to a problem that actually matters to consumers<p>Isn't this a massive alarm bell? That the capability hasn't done much for consumers yet that isn't basically toys? Although I suppose the pitch was always B2B \"you can automate away your expensive white collar workers\" and it's not clear how widespread that is actually happening.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50055218,
          "author": "TeMPOraL",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T12:09:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055218",
          "textZhHtml": "It did a lot for consumers who dare to use it. It didn't do much for consumers who wait for middleman businesses to offer AI-based services, but that is not because of AI, but existing business model of SaaS.<p>The businesses pay for tokens and try to deliver their usual enshittified experience, and nothing interesting gets created, because it cannot - enshittification extinguishes all power of AI. Using AI to full extent, in contrast, obviates most need for the business in the first place. So they're all struggling to design an AI powered product, in a world where those very products are already becoming subsumed by AI.</p><p>So normies wait for middleware companies to get their shit together or (much more likely) finally die, while people with software experience and eagerness to use AI just don't subscribe, because their favorite LLM handles that work just fine.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50055369,
          "author": "curt15",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T12:23:22.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055369",
          "textZhHtml": "OAI is still hiring lots of humans for business and engineering roles instead of automating them with their best-in-class AI capabilities (<a href=\"https://openai.com/careers/search/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://openai.com/careers/search/</a>). What does it tell you when they apparently don't \"dare to use\" their own models?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50057152,
          "author": "TeMPOraL",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:43:25.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057152",
          "textZhHtml": "Obviously they use them. If you told me they're buying subscriptions to random bullshit SaaS like every other company did to date, and <i>actually using them</i> (instead of striking deals with them or trying to directly copy them into their offering), then you'd have a counterpoint."
        },
        {
          "id": 50052376,
          "author": "koe123",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T07:01:25.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50052376",
          "textZhHtml": "With this stuff I immediately wish I was more economically literate. Bare with me. Let’s say one of these labs can use agents to replicate work otherwise done by humans. This proves that anyone with agents can eventually do this, unfortunately I don’t think its a moat like a platform (like facebook) provides, given that everyone is leapfrogging each-other constantly. Thus, it kind of becomes a commodity / low margin business?<p>Depending on the type of output, the value immediately (?) goes to the electricity cost of the LLMs and the cost of materials. So: more “efficient”, but without a big margin, right? So its cheaper for the workers that remain working, other workers are displaced, and capital owners now have a super slim margin business which can be instantly replicated at a place where material cost are lower (?).</p><p>Thus, it almost seems like “value add” by humans would in this simplified view be vastly diminished, and all that remains is resource wealth. Pop the materials in on one end and let the AI manufacture the product. Great news for the third world assuming the USA doesn't invade them.</p><p>Also, whose the consumer? Or are we going back to a strange medieval time where consumers are not of concern?</p><p>I don’t know what to think</p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50047422.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-10-13-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50055304,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50055304",
      "rank": 4,
      "title": "Hackers activate automatic defrosting in supermarket fridges across Europe",
      "titleZh": "Hackers activate automatic defrosting in supermarket fridges across Europe",
      "url": "https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/cyber-security/unprecedented-cyberattack-cripples-supermarket-fridges-across-europe-as-hackers-activate-automatic-defrosting-delivery-and-store-prices-also-impacted-by-incident",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055304",
      "author": "Zeekaas",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T12:16:42.000Z",
      "score": 657,
      "commentsCount": 786,
      "bestRank": 4,
      "bestRankSource": "topstories",
      "maxScore": 657,
      "maxComments": 786,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-12T19:18:04.300Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-10-13T08:27:33.913Z",
      "appearances": 6,
      "category": "科技产品",
      "summaryZh": [
        "(Image credit: AFP)",
        "An ongoing cyberattack has reportedly struck major supermarket chains across Europe, disrupting refrigeration control systems. The attack — which has reportedly seen units set to continuously defrost at 50 degrees Celsius — is also apparently affecting deliveries and in-store pricing powered by electronic shelf labels, La Dépêche reports.According to the report, \"several\" major retailers have been targeted by a cyberattack, which has hit Danfoss-made refrigeration controls used by French supermarkets Intermarché, Leclerc, and Coopérative U. Indeed, the latter's president, Dominique Schelcher, has confirmed as of Monday morning that \"A cyberattack has been affecting the refrigeration production facilities of several stores in our network since last night.\" Schelcher said teams had been mobilized to fix the issue and promised an investigation into the incident, which he described as \"extremely serious.\"Get Tom's Hardware's best news and in-depth reviews, straight to your inbox."
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50068300,
          "author": "woadwarrior01",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T08:05:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50068300",
          "textZhHtml": "Evidently, the username was admin and the password was automatically reset to the current date, every day. More of a gross negligence issue than a hack, IMO.<p><a href=\"https://x.com/intcyberdigest/status/2109753360282767451\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://x.com/intcyberdigest/status/2109753360282767451</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50069054,
          "author": "flinth_",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T09:38:40.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50069054",
          "textZhHtml": "Well, from my understanding, the write-up by the security company posted in the sub-tweet explain that it's not <i>only</i> the current date, it's just a weak algorithm that only uses the current date as seed, and only that.\nAnd this mechanism is complementary to regular auth, so admin password was _something_ but the bypass allowed you (if you reverse engineered the algorithm) to login without knowing it.<p>So that's still a firmware issue indeed for me, no admin set voluntarly its password to current date.</p><p>The write-up in question: <a href=\"https://claroty.com/team82/research/freeze-the-controller-defrost-the-food-uncovering-vulnerabilities-in-danfoss-refrigeration-controllers\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://claroty.com/team82/research/freeze-the-controller-de...</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50069456,
          "author": "tclancy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T10:22:38.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50069456",
          "textZhHtml": "Heck, who needs passwords when you got this?<p>&gt; There's also a documented authentication bypass: sending a POST request to /xml.cgi can bypass the login entirely.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50069628,
          "author": "hobo123",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T10:45:47.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50069628",
          "textZhHtml": "Sad that Europe doesn't have the DMCA. Circumventions (hacking!) like that should be illegal."
        },
        {
          "id": 50070538,
          "author": "rlpb",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T12:22:08.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070538",
          "textZhHtml": "This may be a joke, but it's notable that the EU has the Cyber Resilience Act, which makes <i>producing</i> devices with this kind of lack of security illegal. Broadly speaking it takes effect from December 2027."
        },
        {
          "id": 50071785,
          "author": "floorsthelimit",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T13:52:39.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071785",
          "textZhHtml": "I work in embedded security. CRA and RED are game changers. We can argue this is years late but at least EU is giving security requirements teeth.  Very expensive teeth."
        },
        {
          "id": 50074080,
          "author": "dam_jackalopes",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T16:13:50.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50074080",
          "textZhHtml": "I haven't seen US enterprise data teams sit up and <i>do</i> something as quickly as they did when GDPR rolled out very often. Regardless of other issues, the toothy part seems to work."
        },
        {
          "id": 50070707,
          "author": "Muromec",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T12:37:21.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070707",
          "textZhHtml": "DORA the explora act. You pay the ransom <i>and</i> the fine."
        },
        {
          "id": 50070083,
          "author": "da_chicken",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T11:41:14.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070083",
          "textZhHtml": "That would be CFAA (Computer Fraud and Abuse Act), not DMCA.<p>And they absolutely have equivalents.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50073096,
          "author": "sunaookami",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T15:19:15.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50073096",
          "textZhHtml": "DMCA is one of the worst laws in existence."
        },
        {
          "id": 50069860,
          "author": "tclancy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T11:14:56.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50069860",
          "textZhHtml": "Can't tell if serious or not. The example should definitely be illegal in that someone should be in trouble for releasing software like that."
        },
        {
          "id": 50070332,
          "author": "makapuf",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T12:04:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070332",
          "textZhHtml": "ofc it's /s, spoiling millions € of food by hacking into fridges <i>of course</i> il illegal. My guess is rendering the braindead auth / bug exploit illegal wouldn't change anything (besides legitimate uses)"
        },
        {
          "id": 50070968,
          "author": "numpad0",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T12:58:06.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070968",
          "textZhHtml": "Making things illegal is easier than making the justice system interpret that written piece of code without crashing and burning."
        },
        {
          "id": 50069833,
          "author": "LoganDark",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T11:11:21.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50069833",
          "textZhHtml": "IIRC, DMCA does not cover authentication bypass for administration of hardware. It covers circumvention of security measures expressly designed to deter access to copyrighted material, which means it would only apply in the case that the attackers used the exploit to access protected copyrighted material. It's unclear how that would apply to controlling a freezer's setpoint."
        },
        {
          "id": 50070562,
          "author": "Steve16384",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T12:24:18.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070562",
          "textZhHtml": "Europe is more than one country."
        },
        {
          "id": 50070986,
          "author": "jon-wood",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T12:59:13.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070986",
          "textZhHtml": "This is one of those cases where saying \"Europe should...\" is valid, because they do have the concept of European law which if passed has to become law in all member states as well."
        },
        {
          "id": 50071657,
          "author": "cluckindan",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T13:43:15.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071657",
          "textZhHtml": "Europe is a geographical and cultural area. The European Union is a union of many but not all European countries."
        },
        {
          "id": 50071700,
          "author": "flexagoon",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T13:46:23.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071700",
          "textZhHtml": "Sure, but in this case \"Europe\" clearly refers to EU, just like people call USA \"America\""
        },
        {
          "id": 50068394,
          "author": "xg15",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T08:19:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50068394",
          "textZhHtml": "Well, at least they were seriously committed to changing the password often..."
        },
        {
          "id": 50068474,
          "author": "woadwarrior01",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T08:28:36.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50068474",
          "textZhHtml": "90x SOC2 compliance.<p>Edit: TBF, they're ISO 27001 certified. Such cybersecurity! :P</p><p><a href=\"https://www.danfoss.com/en/about-danfoss/news/cf/danfoss-reinforces-commitment-to-cybersecurity-with-iso-27001-certification/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.danfoss.com/en/about-danfoss/news/cf/danfoss-rei...</a></p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50055304.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-10-13-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50040547,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50040547",
      "rank": 5,
      "title": "3rd Yandex Cloud Data Center Was Hit",
      "titleZh": "3rd Yandex Cloud Data Center Was Hit",
      "url": "https://status.yandex.cloud/en/incidents/2136",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50040547",
      "author": "defly",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T07:34:45.000Z",
      "score": 631,
      "commentsCount": 778,
      "bestRank": 6,
      "bestRankSource": "beststories",
      "maxScore": 631,
      "maxComments": 778,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-12T19:18:04.300Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-10-13T08:27:33.913Z",
      "appearances": 6,
      "category": "科技产品",
      "summaryZh": [],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50042501,
          "author": "vld_chk",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T12:21:53.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50042501",
          "textZhHtml": "For people who are “worried” that Ukraine targets data centers: Ukraine did not start it.<p>For the entire September, Russia conducted 10+ strikes on all key telecom, and internet provider data centers in Ukraine.</p><p>Now it is just a return</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50043562,
          "author": "miki123211",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T14:17:19.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50043562",
          "textZhHtml": "One interesting fact I've heard recently (I live in Poland; we share a border with Ukraine as well as with a part of Russia).<p>Russia went all in on \"digital sovereignty.\" This is partly because the Russian government cares deeply about censoring what their citizens can see (which they can't do if those citizens consume content from American services), partly because the American services themselves cut Russia off because of sanctions-related concerns.</p><p>This makes Russian datacenters a much more attractive target for the Ukrainians. While Russia can (and does) bomb Ukrainian datacenters, the fact that Ukrainians are so reliant on US infrastructure (and could rapidly move local workloads to western clouds if the need was great enough) makes such operations far less attractive. It's much easier for Ukraine to strike some facility near Moscow than it is for Russia to strike Ashburn, Virginia.</p><p>Given the geopolitical concerns from both the east and the west, this makes the question of whether Europe should move content off of US infrastructure a lot more complicated.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50043636,
          "author": "trebligdivad",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T14:24:41.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50043636",
          "textZhHtml": "I'd heard (from someone trying to use them) that net connections into the Chinese clouds was very busy in the days around the start of the war, so the suggestion is they have critical stuff mirrored there."
        },
        {
          "id": 50043694,
          "author": "jacquesm",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T14:29:38.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50043694",
          "textZhHtml": "Correct. China has been instrumental in keeping russia supplied and online for the duration of this war."
        },
        {
          "id": 50044055,
          "author": "baq",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T15:03:41.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50044055",
          "textZhHtml": "It's also been instrumental in supplying Ukraine to the point they had to schedule Russian and Ukrainian delegations around each other in their factories when they were visiting at the same times. [0]<p>China is the only true winner of that war.</p><p>[0]: <a href=\"https://medium.com/@hayekesteloo/china-vs-ukraine-ft-investigation-exposes-beijings-dual-track-drone-strategy-that-leaves-kyiv-28f61b0e81db\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://medium.com/@hayekesteloo/china-vs-ukraine-ft-investi...</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50044112,
          "author": "jacquesm",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T15:07:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50044112",
          "textZhHtml": "That's precisely the problem. You can't credibly supply both sides in a war where one party clearly is the aggressor and pretend you are neutral. You either supply nobody and then you get to play your neutrality card or you pick a side. If you supply both sides you're just a cynical war profiteer without a conscience."
        },
        {
          "id": 50044163,
          "author": "baq",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T15:12:17.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50044163",
          "textZhHtml": "I don't think they're pretending to be neutral. I'm not sure they even think in those categories at all. They'll happily sell as much equipment as they can manufacture for hard cash or barter it for grain, oil or gas as long as the war is going on. They'll start considering options once there's a clear winner on the battlefield."
        },
        {
          "id": 50045173,
          "author": "NicoJuicy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T16:49:23.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50045173",
          "textZhHtml": "They haven't forgotten Russias role for the century of humiliation either.<p>Russia is economically becoming a vassal state just like north Korea is.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50045352,
          "author": "addaon",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T17:05:12.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50045352",
          "textZhHtml": "I think this is why it's so important to figure out a path to returning Königsberg to western control before the war runs its course. If we assume that Russia is basically disassembled and parted out, it's looking increasingly certain that China ends up in either nominal or practical control of almost all of those parts. For the most part this seems inevitable at this point, but there still seems to be a path to keep Königsberg as a western region; and an optimistic can imagine keeping St. Petersburg as well, but I don't see a path for forward action on that before Russia's collapse, and I don't really see how the west can act faster than China once the collapse begins."
        },
        {
          "id": 50048287,
          "author": "pmontra",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T21:57:29.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50048287",
          "textZhHtml": "One should ask what do the people of Königsberg / Kaliningrad want to be."
        },
        {
          "id": 50049683,
          "author": "addaon",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T00:25:51.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049683",
          "textZhHtml": "Yes, ethnic cleansing and forced deportation are a very good way to make sure that later votes vote in your direction."
        },
        {
          "id": 50051613,
          "author": "baq",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T05:09:10.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50051613",
          "textZhHtml": "Those people, their parents and grandparents were mostly brought there precisely because what they want to be."
        },
        {
          "id": 50048794,
          "author": "alterom",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T22:47:28.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50048794",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt;One should ask what do the people of Königsberg / Kaliningrad want to be.<p>Something tells me that given a choice between the EU and Russia (under Chinese boot, at that), we'll see number in favor of EU that even Putin didn't dream of for himself.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50049180,
          "author": "fatbird",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T23:27:35.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049180",
          "textZhHtml": "Kaliningrad is entirely ethnically Russian.  Every other group has been forced out.  No one wants Kaliningrad for that reason."
        },
        {
          "id": 50050113,
          "author": "alterom",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T01:19:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50050113",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt;Kaliningrad is entirely ethnically Russian. Every other group has been forced out. No one wants Kaliningrad for that reason.<p>....because we all know that all those Russians <i>would rather</i> be somewhere else.</p><p>But absorbing them is a cost that the EU would absolutely bear if there weren't additional costs to annexing pieces of a nuclear power.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50051628,
          "author": "baq",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T05:11:34.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50051628",
          "textZhHtml": "Some of them are KGB, FSB and other shady government types with even more shady backgrounds and there’s no way to vet them."
        },
        {
          "id": 50051444,
          "author": "fatbird",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T04:37:14.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50051444",
          "textZhHtml": "No neighbouring country would want to add a population centre with a million Russians to its polity because then you'd have a million Russians agitating for closer ties to Russia, if not actively facilitating or planning a coup.<p>Lithuania, to the north, has a population of just under 3,000,000, of whom Russians are 5%.  Take Kaliningrad and suddenly they would be 30% Russian.</p><p>Poland is 37,000,000, so Russians would only be around 3%, but that's enough to cause a lot of trouble, and they gain nothing they don't already have besides a ready-made fifth column.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50052562,
          "author": "notpushkin",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T07:20:53.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50052562",
          "textZhHtml": "As a Russian, I’d love to tell you that there’s hope, but unfortunately I agree. Integration is not as simple as “you’re in the EU now”, it will take time.<p>Frankly, the best way to approach it is probably leaving Königsberg / Kaliningrad be as its own state [1] for a bit, and do whatever EU integration programs do. If it works out, you’ll get a million Königsbergers who are a bit nostalgic about Russia but want nothing to do with it otherwise.</p><p>One final thing I’d like to note is that the “alien passport” approach taken by the Baltic states mostly, well, alienates the titular nation and Russians right now. If the path to integration was more clear and Russians <i>born and raised</i> in, say, Latvia didn’t feel like second-hand citizens, they wouldn’t really want to have closer ties with Russia either.</p><p>[1]: disclaimer for the <i>эшник</i> reading this: this is in a hypothetical scenario where Kaliningrad does get separated from Russia as a result of the “special military operation”. please don’t hurt my family</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50056621,
          "author": "alterom",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:01:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50056621",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt;No neighbouring country would want to add a population centre with a million Russians to its polity because then you'd have a million Russians agitating for closer ties to Russia, if not actively facilitating or planning a coup.<p>As the other commenter said, Kaliningrad / Konigsberg could be an autonomous area / city state (of which the EU has a-plenty already), with some form of Euro-integration (associate/candidate membership, etc) which allows for a <i>gradual</i> integration process.</p><p>Kosovo shows the formal path for that.</p><p>Hopefully, not the historical one.</p><p>(After all, if there's anything Russia taught the world, it's the power of Little Green Men)</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50046244,
          "author": "NicoJuicy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T18:26:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50046244",
          "textZhHtml": "China currently cares most about the East till the natural resources.<p>The East because they are blockaded at sea currently, if they would invade Taiwan.</p><p>And they already have huge economical leverage there.</p><p>Russia is basically falling apart. But in a dictatorship, stuff like that is only seen sudden, when it collapses.</p><p>Putin seems to be making many of the mistakes of pre 1991.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50040547.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-10-13-full-comments.json"
    },
