      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50044079,
          "author": "dekhn",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T15:05:33.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50044079",
          "textZhHtml": "I work in drug discovery and AI and his slide about cancer medicine isn't very well informed.  We have models and narratives about how drugs work, but they are woefully incomplete.<p>I ask nearly every doctor/researched in the medical field: if you had a AI-created drug that tremendously improved cancer treatment outcomes for your patient, would you hesitate to prescribe it because nobody understood how it worked?   I have yet to hear \"yes, I would hesitate\", most people say \"it would be cruel to deny a person a treatment that worked\".</p><p>I think Tao is focusing too much on second order effects of AI on math and other fields; humans are <i>terrible</i> at reasoning about second order effects, especially ones that are happening dynamically, in real time, using the most advanced mathematical models the world has yet created.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50046850,
          "author": "captainclam",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T19:21:48.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50046850",
          "textZhHtml": "I also work in drug discovery, and I completely agree; the extent to which the actual causal mechanisms of the efficacy of many drugs is woefully short of some human-appreciable first-principles based explanation. The point is especially undercut by hypothetically suggesting that this drug /does/ in fact pass stage 3 trials, at which point I can't fathom anyone would have a problem advancing it.<p>Really wish he had chosen a different example; this particular bullet point has been making the rounds on X/twitter to paint Tao as an example of some sort of gatekeeping luddite who would deny the world a post-abundance future in order to maintain the prestige of his particular career path...which is tough because I cannot think of a more responsible steward of our inevitable AI future than Tao at the moment.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50049436,
          "author": "2muchcoffeeman",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T23:53:39.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049436",
          "textZhHtml": "I think his last point is important<p>* Could the AI solution be somehow misaligned by exploiting\na weakness in the trial process or its math models?*</p><p>Maybe it’s a clumsy example but I think the difference is humans would follow the spirit of the trial process.</p><p>Could an AI find flaws in the process and then use them to get its drug passed? I think that’s the question.</p><p>Are you saying that the trail process is good enough that we shouldn’t worry about it?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50055433,
          "author": "zaphar",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T12:28:30.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055433",
          "textZhHtml": "I'm not necessarily an AI booster but I don't think it's at all true that \"humans would follow the spirit of the trial process\" Some of them will. But I've met far too many humans in all areas of life who will happily follow the letter and not the spirit. Sometimes out of malicious compliance, sometimes out of sheer exhaustion, sometimes because they can't tell the difference between the letter and the spirit and sometimes for a whole host of other reasons like laziness. There will be a not insignificant number of humans are not at this vary moment following the spirit of the thing."
        },
        {
          "id": 50063590,
          "author": "2muchcoffeeman",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T21:55:27.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50063590",
          "textZhHtml": "Sure, we need redundancies and checks and balances in the system. You can’t trust everyone.<p>It’s not as though people who cheat the system aren’t reported or found out. Academics that lie in their research and companies that use loop holes are found out all the time. Maybe there is no will in some countries to do anything about it, but they are found out.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50064165,
          "author": "dekhn",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T22:50:00.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064165",
          "textZhHtml": "The important bit is that clinical trials already have those redundancies and checks and balances, and Tao didn't even bother to learn about those before going straight to reward hacking.<p>Academia and biotech is rife with reward hacking, I think the math people just don't get out enough to understand that entire fields have built up massive regulatory structures and lobbying systems around this already.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50056890,
          "author": "Georgelemental",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:23:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50056890",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; humans would follow the spirit of the trial process<p>Yes. Pharmaceutical companies, famous for their sterling integrity, would never ever try to game the system. Pure as driven snow, they are!</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50063461,
          "author": "2muchcoffeeman",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T21:43:18.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50063461",
          "textZhHtml": "Which is why it’s not just up to a drug company to self approve itself."
        },
        {
          "id": 50065559,
          "author": "yunwal",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T01:48:04.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065559",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; humans would follow the spirit of the trial process<p>P-hacking has been a problem well before LLMs were wearing diapers</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50055315,
          "author": "criley2",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T12:18:39.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055315",
          "textZhHtml": "Remember, the vast majority of pivotal Phase 3 trials are funded by for-profit companies.<p>Twenty years ago, Rick Ng's Drugs: From Discovery to Approval cited benchmarks of roughly $1 billion, 10 years, and 10,000 candidates to get a single drug across the line. Today, estimates easily run $2 billion to $4 billion.</p><p>Given that level of financial pressure, especially in Phase 3 where a single failure can wipe out a biotech company, assuming humans \"follow the spirit of the trial process\" is wishful thinking. Humans already exploit the system through p-hacking, shifting surrogate endpoints, and cherry-picking subgroups. An AI gaming trial parameters wouldn't be a new problem. It would just be an automated version of what sponsors already do.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50047987,
          "author": "curt15",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T21:28:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50047987",
          "textZhHtml": "The accounts spreading that narrative on X no doubt have their own agenda. Before people start hyperventilating about \"AI\" conquering human endeavors, remember that the cash-strapped frontier labs are themselves still hiring human \"Account Associates\" and \"Android Engineers\" instead of saving those salaries using their own AI capabilities.<p><a href=\"https://openai.com/careers/search/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://openai.com/careers/search/</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50048629,
          "author": "Loquebantur",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T22:32:44.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50048629",
          "textZhHtml": "Tao actually pictures the AI as having hacked the FDA, so as to pass the test despite the drug being lethal.<p>Picking out that one example and misrepresenting it on top of it indeed clearly is driven by an agenda. Or mere stupidity.</p><p>The central point in all of this is the misguided idea of \"letting AI think for us\". An over-generalization feeding into the society-wide problem of self-infantilization.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50050306,
          "author": "dekhn",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T01:47:13.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50050306",
          "textZhHtml": "I think the best thing to say is that by thinking through the example, sharing it with people in the field, and then steelmanning it, he might have had some good points:\n1) pharma would love to understand the mechanisms of drugs in detail but we don't have that.  AI could help.\n2) there is a possibility that a clinical trial run by AI could hack the trial get something approved when it wasn't safe or effective."
        },
        {
          "id": 50065579,
          "author": "yunwal",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T01:51:16.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065579",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; there is a possibility that a clinical trial run by AI could hack the trial get something approved when it wasn't safe or effective<p>This possibility already exists and nothing about AI fundamentally changes it</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50065757,
          "author": "dekhn",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T02:14:48.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065757",
          "textZhHtml": "Yes, obviously.  Everybody here who works in pharma or is familiar with clinical trials and their history knows this."
        },
        {
          "id": 50047000,
          "author": "calf",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T19:38:01.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50047000",
          "textZhHtml": "The conflations about his example (\"thought experiment\") is twofold, the AI itself confounds the ethical intuition so it is wrong directly compare against what real medicine today does; furthermore, the very admission that real medicine in fact operates through unknown risks (e.g. Jannsen vaccine recall) is different than having a scientific standard that it should not have to be that way at least as minimally as possible."
        },
        {
          "id": 50049205,
          "author": "gyanchawdhary",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T23:30:03.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049205",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt;to paint Tao as an example of some sort of gatekeeping luddite who would deny the world a post-abundance future in order to maintain the prestige of his particular career path<p>why is it so hard to accept that a brilliant mathematician can have the same human flaws as anyone else i.e. ego, arrogance, jealousy and competitiveness?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50057974,
          "author": "kenferry",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T15:37:15.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057974",
          "textZhHtml": "it's not hard to accept in general, but have you been following Tao for long? The combination of how virtuous he is with how informed he is with how smart he is is pretty special. He's also far from a luddite. He's been a leader of the computer-math charge.<p>Questions of AI policy are all hard and anyone who confidently says they know the right thing to do is too confident. Tao's fallible like anyone else, but yeah, as voices to pay attention to go he's very good.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50058925,
          "author": "gyanchawdhary",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T16:35:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50058925",
          "textZhHtml": "Neil armstrong too should have been listened to .. and just like Tao .. he was quite virtuous, and informed and pretty special .. wont you say so ? and YET he was very vocal about expressing his skepticism about spacex before congress .. thank god his views were rejected .. or we might have missed the magnificent (and now normalised) sight of rockets landing vertically and the incredible engineering behind catching that beast of rocket with chopsticks etc etc<p>what im suprised about though is why arnt you and others here more suspectful of Tao's skeptisim of AI .. as Wittgenstein's ruler reminds us teh very thing we use to measure something else may itself need measuring ..  so why should tao's expertise be any exception?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50062185,
          "author": "kenferry",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T20:03:30.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50062185",
          "textZhHtml": "I'm not saying Tao is right, but (a) he knows a LOT more about about what's actually important in research math than I do (and probably than you do) (b) he's not just having a knee jerk reaction. Tao has been a major leader and advocate for computer-assisted math, including AI, and I don't think that has really changed. He's advocating for how to do it well. He's far from an AI skeptic protecting his turf. Is there any other person as well-versed in research mathematics AND AI-math as Tao? Maybe a few, but not many.<p>The dude keeps a continually automatically produced document of his takes on AI. He's not a skeptic overall. <a href=\"https://teorth.github.io/tao-web/ai-views.html\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://teorth.github.io/tao-web/ai-views.html</a> . In this interview the interviewer describes him as an evangelist for AI in math and he doesn't disagree. <a href=\"https://teorth.github.io/tao-web/ai-views-interview.html\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://teorth.github.io/tao-web/ai-views-interview.html</a></p><p>It seems like your inclination is to discount his experience with research mathematics. I agree with you that that's probably what Taleb would do - Taleb has a giant ego and basically operates under the assumption that everyone else has the same giant ego as he does but is (a) less honest about it (b) dumber than he is.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50043026.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-10-13-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50048642,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50048642",
      "rank": 7,
      "title": "Fingerprints are the new cookies",
      "titleZh": "Fingerprints are the new cookies",
      "url": "https://royfish.dev/fingerprints-are-the-new-cookies",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50048642",
      "author": "royfish",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T22:33:39.000Z",
      "score": 717,
      "commentsCount": 354,
      "bestRank": 2,
      "bestRankSource": "beststories",
      "maxScore": 717,
      "maxComments": 354,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-12T19:18:04.300Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-10-13T08:27:33.913Z",
      "appearances": 6,
      "category": "科技产品",
      "summaryZh": [
        "From now on I’ll call you:",
        "… computing…"
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50053455,
          "author": "alkonaut",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T09:01:22.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50053455",
          "textZhHtml": "I still don't understand why the highest entropy APIs aren't gated behind browser consent.<p>A news site doesn't need to do audio processing on my machine and read back the buffer? It doesn't need to use webgl to render triangles to an offscreen buffer?</p><p>Broad geographical targeting shouldn't be an issue. In fact, it's so easy that I think we should invent an ad-targeting API people can opt in to. If I could tell advertisers my geographic location, gender, age cohort, languages, and 3 interests, I'd not feel that's an invasion of privacy when they use that to deliver ads. I'd just get lawnmower ads instead of shampoo ads and that's fine.</p><p>The problem is, they don't want to use that kind of targeting.\nThey want to target me with ads for a travel destination that my coworker mentioned in a Messenger message to another coworker five minutes ago. But we should just not accept that.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50060282,
          "author": "fg137",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T18:01:15.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50060282",
          "textZhHtml": "The most used browser comes from the same company that runs the largest ad network.<p>It's the browser that introduced many of these APIs in the first place.</p><p>That should answer the question.</p><p>(If you ask me if a browser should report how many cores the machine has, including physical and logical ones, I would answer never, every time. But not everyone thinks that way. <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Navigator/hardwareConcurrency\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Navigator/h...</a>)</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50054481,
          "author": "rickdeckard",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T10:54:11.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054481",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; <i>\"I still don't understand why the highest entropy APIs aren't gated behind browser consent\"</i><p>Because it's known that interrupting the user-intent with a unrelated consent-popup doesn't help.</p><p>Older people may remember that this was the approach of Java J2ME, where you couldn't provide a fluent user-experience as the user first had to answer huge popups about individual API-permissions with \"Always Allow\", \"Allow now\" etc.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50055139,
          "author": "fireflash38",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T12:03:19.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055139",
          "textZhHtml": "If it isn't an informed decision, it isn't really a decision at all. And the huge majority of people the huge majority of the time do not want to make an informed decision: they want what they came to the site for, news, games, family photos, etc.<p>You go to Facebook to see your cute nephew make spit bubbles. Do you care that they access your camera and canvas? What is a canvas? And sure access my camera, I want to upload photos!</p><p>It's fundamentally broken. They've studied the dark patterns, not to avoid them but to implement them best.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50058540,
          "author": "tyromaniac",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T16:14:00.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50058540",
          "textZhHtml": "Absolutely. I spoke to an ex-Meta guy and his only answer to stuff like this was \"actually its good for privacy because they don't want third parties to access your data since its valuable\""
        },
        {
          "id": 50054523,
          "author": "alkonaut",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T10:59:27.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054523",
          "textZhHtml": "So long as it's once per API and site, it seems it doesn't need to be too bad?<p>If it's a site I trust, and I think it genuinely needs audio, I give it audio. If someone doesn't understand and just does allow all, they never see it again either (and their ads are suspiciously well targeted).</p><p>We already have these consent popups for camera and microphone. Reading back bytes from an audio buffer seems like such a rare thing to do that it hopefully just stops happening.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50055837,
          "author": "routerl",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T13:02:07.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055837",
          "textZhHtml": "It's less rare than you'd hope. Disclosure: I do developer relations at Friendly Captcha, and our widget is one of the scripts that uses generated offline audio (but with no microphone recording) and reads the buffer back on sites that otherwise have no use for audio.<p>We do this to check whether the browser is what it claims to be, not to track who you are. Similarly, font rendering, floating-point math results, WebGL renderer strings, and whether built-in functions are patched is all much harder to fake. These considerations determine how hard the proof-of-work puzzle should be, and site owners can see it as a risk score. We don't do cookies or browser storage, IPs aren't stored, and nothing gets used to recognize you on another site or on a later visit. The biggest players in automated traffic prevention can't (and usually don't) say this much, because of conflicts of interest between automated bot detection and their other products (e.g. ad sales and IP-profiling).</p><p>Your example is really fair though. I chatted with our engineers, prompted by this thread, and we found nothing in our detection that currently uses the audio hashes we collect, so we've started talking about removing the collection.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50056398,
          "author": "alkonaut",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T13:45:20.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50056398",
          "textZhHtml": "I think \"proof of humanity\" is going to be an increasingly important thing, but I think we need to forego the age-verification and make some sort of zero-proof/integrity-preserving age/humanity verification."
        },
        {
          "id": 50060471,
          "author": "qurren",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T18:14:55.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50060471",
          "textZhHtml": "Also, people not in tech do not generally understand the implication of consent popups.<p>For example, on multiple instances, my spouse declined \"location permissions\" an app designed to work with a piece of Bluetooth-connected hardware, and would blame the hardware as being crappy.</p><p>\"This smart lock app doesn't work.\"</p><p>\"You need to enable this permission.\"</p><p>\"Why do I need to give the smart lock app location permissions?\"</p><p>It's not obvious to people that \"Bluetooth can be used sneakily by big-enough companies as a makeshift GPS\" even if the app needing Bluetooth access doesn't actually need a GPS location.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50062222,
          "author": "floorsthelimit",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T20:05:52.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50062222",
          "textZhHtml": "Your partner isn’t wrong. It’s a crappy product and crappy ecosystem when you have to be hyper precise."
        },
        {
          "id": 50054411,
          "author": "Panzerschrek",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T10:47:11.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054411",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; A news site doesn't need to do audio processing on my machine and read back the buffer? It doesn't need to use webgl to render triangles to an offscreen buffer?<p>We should go further. A web-site with mostly static content should not use user-side scripting (Javascript) at all, it's unnecessary for it. It should be required only in rare cases where a site is basically an application and require manually turning it on each time such application is opened.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50054482,
          "author": "alkonaut",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T10:54:36.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054482",
          "textZhHtml": "I think that ship sailed and I'm pragmatic enough to accept it. The web is an application platform, not a web of hypertext content. That's not going to change, and I've given up on the idea to even try to \"go back\" there. If it worked, you'd just have half of websites be \"sites\" that work without JS, and half would be \"apps\" which would require switching on JS? The whole reaosn the web took off as it has is because we removed the distinction between \"web app\" and \"web site\". There's no putting that genie back in the bottle."
        },
        {
          "id": 50054669,
          "author": "jcgl",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T11:14:46.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054669",
          "textZhHtml": "Sure, for the most static of static sites. But without JavaScript you can’t even do basic things client-side, such as having some variable-length list of fields in a form. Or customized field validation.<p>If such client-side behavior counts as “basically an application,” then “applications” is a larger set than seems useful.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50063905,
          "author": "TheFlyingFish",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T22:23:37.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50063905",
          "textZhHtml": "Theme switchers! My favorite example of why \"document-only\" sites might need JS. Sure, you can use @media prefers-color-scheme for defaults, and you can do checkbox/radio shenanigans to make it switchable, but there's no way to persist that preference on a static site without JS.<p>If you don't care about your site being static then you can track it server side, but that has big implications for his you can just your site. Sticking a little flag in localStorage really is the best solution, if you can get past ideological purity.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50066507,
          "author": "jcgl",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T04:01:27.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50066507",
          "textZhHtml": "Good example. There is simply a long tail of things that are valuable to static sites that HTML can't/won't provide. Some other examples from my simple blog:<p>- Using buttons/links to seek to specific times in a &lt;video&gt;</p><p>- Localizing dates</p><p>- Expanding/collapsing sets of comments</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50057084,
          "author": "account42",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:38:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057084",
          "textZhHtml": "Most sites also don't need forms at all either, at least for normal users."
        },
        {
          "id": 50058251,
          "author": "usefulcat",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T15:55:37.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50058251",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; A web-site with mostly static content should not use user-side scripting (Javascript) at all<p>Then sites that want to track will just add some JS. Seems unlikely to help.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50053897,
          "author": "fc417fc802",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T09:49:25.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50053897",
          "textZhHtml": "Well yeah, but there's also the part where I'm not okay loading ads from a third party because they eat my battery, add latency, and can potentially be used to spread malware. So there's the conflicting preferences regarding targeting that you mention but also conflicting preferences regarding how they are served."
        },
        {
          "id": 50057110,
          "author": "account42",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:40:14.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057110",
          "textZhHtml": "The conflicting interest starts with the very idea of ads already. The entire point of ads is to manipulate you into doing things you wouldn't do otherwise."
        },
        {
          "id": 50059795,
          "author": "fc417fc802",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T17:30:36.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50059795",
          "textZhHtml": "I suppose that depends on the ad. I don't mind non-intrusive ads that are directly relevant to whatever I'm currently doing. What google currently does with product placement in AI responses is a good example. It's on topic, unobtrusive, and secure."
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50048642.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-10-13-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50028275,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50028275",
      "rank": 8,
      "title": "REA Reverse – Engineer Anything",
      "titleZh": "REA Reverse – Engineer Anything",
      "url": "https://rea.tools/",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50028275",
      "author": "modinfo",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T00:37:07.000Z",
      "score": 783,
      "commentsCount": 329,
      "bestRank": 118,
      "bestRankSource": "topstories",
      "maxScore": 783,
      "maxComments": 329,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-12T19:18:04.300Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-10-12T19:18:04.300Z",
      "appearances": 1,
      "category": "AI",
      "summaryZh": [
        "Reverse engineering, with your coding agent Find out howsoftware works. REA gives your agent the tools to inspect a program and explain what it does. Already know RE? Skip to analysis guides → Want to go deeper? Read the Blog → Set up REA Copy this into your coding agent: Install REA and connect it to this coding agent using npx rea-agents@latest setup. Show me the setup plan for approval, then verify the installation. Or run this in your terminal: npx rea-agents@latest setup Approve the setup plan, then restart your agent. First time, or missing Node.js or a coding agent? Follow the step-by-step setup guide →",
        "What is reverse engineering? Finding out how software works by examining the program itself. The goal: understand a feature well enough to explain it, change it or rebuild it. One question: why does Calculator give 220 for 200 + 10%? Before REA · You investigate it yourself 0x180124945: MOV EAX, dword ptr [R13 + 0x18] 0x180124949: CMP EAX, 0x5c 0x18012494c: JZ 0x180124aba 0x180124952: CMP EAX, 0x5b 0x180124955: JZ 0x180124aba 0x18012495b: MOV EDX, 0x64 0x180124960: LEA RCX, [RSP + 0x160] 0x180124968: CALL 0x180122650 0x18012496e: LEA RDX, [R13 + 0x160] 0x180124975: LEA RCX, [RSP + 0x78] 0x18012497a: CALL 0x180109720 0x18012497f: MOV RSI, RAX 0x180124982: MOV qword ptr [RSP + 0x28], RAX 0x180124987: LEA RDX, [RSP + 0x160] 0x18012498f: MOV RCX, RAX 0x180124992: CALL 0x180123190 ; … value-copy instructions omitted … 0x180124a14: MOV RDX, RDI 0x180124a17: LEA RCX, [RSP + 0xf8] 0x180124a1f: CALL 0x180109720 0x180124a24: LEA R8, [RSP + 0x30] 0x180124a29: MOV RDX, RAX 0x180124a2c: LEA RCX, [RSP + 0xb8] 0x180124a34: CALL 0x180123c10 You decode the instructions, follow the calls and reconstruct the calculation. With REA · You ask your agent One prompt. Ask your agent in plain English. Use REA to inspect Windows Calculator. Why does 200 + 10% give 220? Example answer · based on the findings After +, the % button takes a percentage of the first number. 10% of 200 = 20200 + 20 = 220 REA supplies The handler’s instructions, decompiled code and calls. Your agent explains Which branch applies, what it calculates and how to rebuild it. Selected assembly from the installed Calculator DLL. The thoughts and reply illustrate the findings. Continue with the Calculator example ↓"
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50029703,
          "author": "InvisibleUp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T04:57:03.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50029703",
          "textZhHtml": "Glancing at the Touhou 4 decomp[1], it's a lot better quality than a lot of AI decomps I've seen. It's matching, the variables are named sensibly, comments are sparse and comprehensible, and there's not much in the way of unaddressed Ghidra jank. And only in a month! My main complaint is that the file structuring seems more optimized for AI use than for mirroring the original intent of the devs. (Compare to this human-made Touhou 6 decomp.[2])<p>I know the retro game modding/decomp scene is already getting hit with a flood of low-effort ports.[3] Now they're going to be hit with a flood of half-decent decomps effortlessly generated by anyone with a $200/mo AI subscription, which become half-decent PC ports, which get modding support grafted on. It kinda just... totally eliminates that as a hobby entirely. I know people are going to be upset about this. And it's not even like with the AI art, where the pro and anti-AI camps live in their own camps. Solving the same puzzle twice just feels discouraging. Very similar to the problem academics are having with AI math/physics proofs.</p><p>I know a large reason people hate AI is because it's largely seen as tearing apart hobbyist/professional communities, eliminating reasons to collaborate with others and form relationships, and replacing it with an individualized dependence on a commercial product. It follows the same trend the tech industry took social media, from a method of connection to a tool of propaganda and paranoia and \"@gork is this true?\" This project, I think, is one of the most clear examples of that.</p><p>[1] <a href=\"https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th04\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th04</a>\n[2] <a href=\"https://github.com/GensokyoClub/th06\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/GensokyoClub/th06</a>\n[3] <a href=\"https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/pc-ports-of-old-console-games-are-the-new-ai-vibe-coding-battleground/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/pc-ports-of-old-cons...</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50030093,
          "author": "mjr00",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T06:06:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50030093",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Now they're going to be hit with a flood of half-decent decomps effortlessly generated by anyone with a $200/mo AI subscription, which become half-decent PC ports, which get modding support grafted on. It kinda just... totally eliminates that as a hobby entirely.<p>It really depends on your perspective of what the point of the hobby is. Were decomps/recomps a hobby because people wanted to make sure their old games could run on modern hardware and be extended with mods etc., or were they a hobby because people enjoyed the actual process of using Ghidra and reverse engineering by hand, and the decompiled game was just a side effect?</p><p>Personally I think it's awesome that PC98 Touhou games are getting recomps. Being able to play the series natively on modern hardware, and all hardware for the foreseeable future, is great and preserves the legacy of the series. I understand that for anyone who enjoys the actual act of reverse engineering it feels like cheating, though.</p><p>I think people will get over it. Music is a good example. Drum VSTs and piano VSTs are going to sound way better than you unless you're a 99th percentile musician playing on a 99th percentile physical instrument. But people still play the piano and drums, because it's fun. Same idea; the existence of AI doesn't stop people who love the act of reverse engineering from doing it.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50030327,
          "author": "cassonmars",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T06:42:18.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50030327",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; It really depends on your perspective of what the point of the hobby is. Were decomps/recomps a hobby because people wanted to make sure their old games could run on modern hardware and be extended with mods etc., or were they a hobby because people enjoyed the actual process of using Ghidra and reverse engineering by hand, and the decompiled game was just a side effect?<p>I think your question would make sense if these were the only two reasons. Someone who just wants to make sure their old games can run would see these tools as a good thing. People who just enjoy reverse engineering by hand wouldn't care this exists, or maybe even, a helpful tutor.</p><p>So perhaps then, the sentiment being as negative as it is, is revealing something about the hobby: that many participants, considered the metagame of being the first to do it, to be \"smart enough\" to have figured it out, the \"street cred\" if you were, is what they are lamenting, but they just either lack the words, or aren't being honest with themselves.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50031173,
          "author": "alberto-m",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T09:33:09.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50031173",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; <i>So perhaps then, the sentiment being as negative as it is, is revealing something about the hobby: that many participants, considered the metagame of being the first to do it, to be \"smart enough\" to have figured it out, the \"street cred\"</i><p>This is a very ingenerous interpretation.</p><p>I am a hobby hiker. My mountain hikes are so easy that basically anybody could do them: no street cred involved, it’s not even worth to speak about them. But it is important to me that most goals are only reachable by walking and putting in the effort: if a company offered a 20$/mo service that brings subscribers to the endpoint, carried in a luxury litter by robotized carriers, I would feel that my hobby has been devoided of sense.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50032700,
          "author": "selestify",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T13:22:29.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50032700",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; if a company offered a 20$/mo service that brings subscribers to the endpoint, carried in a luxury litter by robotized carriers, I would feel that my hobby has been devoided of sense.<p>As someone else who hikes: why? I do not understand. Do you not enjoy the hobby of hiking for the sake of hiking?</p><p>The views will be just as beautiful, the air just as fresh, and the feeling of moving my legs just as energizing, regardless of how others may decide to get to the destination themselves. Why would the experience of others have any impact on my own experience of a trail?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50032765,
          "author": "nkrisc",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T13:29:55.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50032765",
          "textZhHtml": "Might be stretching the analogy too far, but look what happens to remote places that become easily accessible. They become commodified, crowded, and polluted.<p>There is something about a place requiring effort to visit that makes it special, and it loses that quality when it can be trivially accessed. The air isn’t quite as fresh when there’s a parking lot there.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50033550,
          "author": "k__o",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T14:50:17.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50033550",
          "textZhHtml": "yes, gatekeeping has much more merit than given credit for. ive seen many communities, ideas, companies, online and off that have been ruined by their growth past a certain point. ive wondered if it's a \"when everyone's special no one is\" thing but maybe it's just a primal human community thing. idk"
        },
        {
          "id": 50038352,
          "author": "jonhohle",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T00:01:17.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50038352",
          "textZhHtml": "I think it’s purely a community size issue. When a community is small, everyone’s an owner. Dealing with bad actors is straightforward. When it grows into the hundreds or thousands the scale means you can’t know everyone anymore, you need hall monitors, need to be more guarded and intentional about how you communicate.<p>It’s not bad or wrong, just different. When the changes forced by a group who didn’t build it, it’s frustrating.</p><p>“Hey, here’s this thing you love doing, but now a computer does it and you don’t have to any more. See, same!” Thanks for allowing me to not do the thing I like to do?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50033568,
          "author": "Peaches4Rent",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T14:52:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50033568",
          "textZhHtml": "I think this is spot on. It's the journey, but its also the destination."
        },
        {
          "id": 50034540,
          "author": "plorkyeran",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T16:36:05.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50034540",
          "textZhHtml": "There is indeed something special about getting to the top of a mountain and knowing that anyone you encounter there went through the same thing you did to get there."
        },
        {
          "id": 50033556,
          "author": "czbc",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T14:51:09.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50033556",
          "textZhHtml": "\"I hope things will stay the same when other people come in but I'm acknowledging they will not\"<p>Rather its the impact of others on, not their experience of, a space that would impact my own experience, for better or worse.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50031793,
          "author": "verandaguy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T11:21:17.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50031793",
          "textZhHtml": "Thanks for framing this in a way that I've struggled to phrase.<p>At every turn it seems like a lot of intellectual pursuits are just being voided by this technology.</p><p>I've heard the \"one level up\" argument in software with the supposition being that the work's still there, it's just becoming more abstract... but even in software, this doesn't really track.</p><p>The injection of this technology into not just intellectual but artistic pursuits (since there are now commercial AI image and music generators) should be a red alert.</p><p>What is there to live for once everything you once loved to do has been optimized for maximum revenue? Where every time you do something for fun, someone will come out of the woodwork when you show it off to either tell you AI could've done it quicker, or that they made an AI version with a flashier UI or something?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50033573,
          "author": "brandonmenc",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T14:52:51.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50033573",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; At every turn it seems like a lot of intellectual pursuits are just being voided by this technology.<p>If the goal was to show off your accomplishments on social media, sure.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50031989,
          "author": "ninjagoo",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T11:52:42.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50031989",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; What is there to live for once everything you once loved to do has been optimized for maximum revenue?<p>if it brings you joy, what does it matter how someone else does it?</p><p>If you run for fun, what does it matter that someone else does it faster, or in a paid exhibition optimized for max revenue?</p><p>Do it because it brings <i>you</i> joy, there is no external dependency.</p><p>&gt; Where every time you do something for fun, someone will come out of the woodwork when you show it off</p><p>See, this is where things get tricky, either the activity brings you joy, and/or the sharing of it brings you joy, and/or the showing off of it brings you joy.</p><p>For the first two, it is irrelevant what someone else has to say or do about it; ignore the noise. For showing off, yes, some activities are no longer worthy, so change is needed to find those that are.</p><p>Hopefully for a hobby, or for anything that you love to do, showing off is not a major criterion for why you do it.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50032372,
          "author": "verandaguy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T12:41:33.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50032372",
          "textZhHtml": "Showing off <i>can</i> be the point. If I did something tricky and creative and got an interesting (possibly even useful!) result, why shouldn't I be proud enough of that to have a \"hey, look at this cool shit I did/made\" moment?<p>That aside, I've been lucky enough in my career to work alongside, mostly, people who have a personal interest in using software as a tool, and knowing their toolchains very well, be that a language, a command line, a system architecture, you name it. If you found something cool to do with a tool, you'd share it to disseminate the knowledge. That culture is very quickly dying off, I feel, because LLM-driven work encourages siloing to a degree I don't think gets discussed enough.</p><p>Lastly, using LLMs to write code is its own type of exhausting. I thought I could keep up my hobby by mentally checking out of work and working on projects by hand in my free time, but babysitting the LLM, reviewing the massive corpora of code and documentation it puts out (including those put out by colleagues), and the <i>insane</i> volume of context switches this technology encourages in the workplace makes me want to stay the hell away from computers alltogether if I can help it.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50035433,
          "author": "TeMPOraL",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T18:00:34.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50035433",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; <i>If you run for fun, what does it matter that someone else does it faster, or in a paid exhibition optimized for max revenue?</i><p>This example is actually very clarifying - perhaps <i>almost no one actually runs for fun</i>?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50037574,
          "author": "tough",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T21:54:20.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50037574",
          "textZhHtml": "if you run to be healthier, how does someone doing so faster affect your health"
        },
        {
          "id": 50032146,
          "author": "alberto-m",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-10T12:12:46.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50032146",
          "textZhHtml": "We are not robots and telling people how they should feel and how their mood should not be influenced by this or that is akin to saying to depressed people to just stop being depressed. The divide-and-conquer method you appear to be using is worthless for human psychology.<p>The cases of a niche culture destroyed by dilution are so common that there are essays around it (e.g. <i>Geeks, MOPs, and sociopaths in subculture evolution</i>), so it seems to be a very normal and human thing to happen. Blaming the individual members for failing to feel differently is not going to fix that.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50038444,
          "author": "ninjagoo",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T00:18:07.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50038444",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; We are not robots and telling people how they should feel and how their mood should not be influenced by this or that is akin to saying to depressed people to just stop being depressed. The divide-and-conquer method you appear to be using is worthless for human psychology.<p>I have some bad news for you -- all major religions are predicated on telling people exactly how to feel. Last I checked, these religions are <i>very</i> popular.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50028275.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-10-13-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50059115,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50059115",
      "rank": 9,
      "title": "Muse Wonder",
      "titleZh": "Muse Wonder",
      "url": "https://research.meta.ai/blog/muse-wonder",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50059115",
      "author": "meetpateltech",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T16:48:01.000Z",
      "score": 623,
      "commentsCount": 329,
      "bestRank": 7,
      "bestRankSource": "topstories",
      "maxScore": 623,
      "maxComments": 329,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-12T19:18:04.300Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-10-13T08:27:33.913Z",
      "appearances": 6,
      "category": "AI",
      "summaryZh": [
        "Researchers introduce Muse Wonder and discuss its capabilities. playback position0:00 of 0:00 A frontier model that leads on personal and professional agents Today, we’re excited to introduce the Muse Wonder series with Muse Wonder and Muse Wonder Full, our most capable models that deliver state-of-the-art performance on personal and professional agentic tasks. Together, they mark the next step on our scaling ladder and a major leap toward personal superintelligence. Muse Wonder brings together Active Memory and Active Focus to keep track of what matters to you and work diligently toward what you want to accomplish. Muse Wonder is available today through the Meta Model API and Muse Code, and Muse Wonder Full is available today for Muse Code subscribers. Muse Wonder will soon power experiences in Muse, and will roll out first to subscribers. Muse Wonder will also power artifact generation and processes that require more intelligence. And in the future, we’ll be using Muse Wonder to develop specialized models that we can bring to everyone using Muse.Scaling Toward Personal Superintelligence Fourteen months ago, we began a ground-up overhaul of our artificial intelligence efforts. In that time, we’ve gone from rebuilding our stack from first principles to pushing the frontier.",
        "When we introduced Muse Spark, we outlined our approach to predictable, rigorous scaling. Today’s release builds on that work across pretraining and reinforcement learning, delivering our most capable models yet. Trained at our gigawatt-scale Prometheus data center, Muse Wonder and Muse Wonder Full bring together the work of teams across Meta to deliver frontier capabilities for complex tasks at work and in everyday life — another step toward bringing personal superintelligence to the billions of people who use our products. Frontier Capabilities Model Highlights The Muse Wonder series achieves state-of-the-art results across key benchmarks for personal and professional agents, memory, and coding."
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50059827,
          "author": "atleastoptimal",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T17:32:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50059827",
          "textZhHtml": "I personally have many problems with Meta, but imo the more companies close to the frontier, the better.<p>If only one company were calling all the shots once they hit recursive self-improvement, it would lead to extreme concentration of power, and their values being enforced across the entire industry. With 3-4 companies at the frontier, there is more urge to cooperate to avoid a catastrophic race condition.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50063202,
          "author": "tintor",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T21:17:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50063202",
          "textZhHtml": "The more companies <i>from different countries</i> close to the frontier, the better."
        },
        {
          "id": 50067583,
          "author": "barrenko",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T06:28:50.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50067583",
          "textZhHtml": "Markets don't <i>really</i> care about countries, but there is one country which the markets seem to disproportionately like."
        },
        {
          "id": 50071730,
          "author": "Matl",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T13:49:03.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071730",
          "textZhHtml": "I think the point is if there's some law in a given country i.e. banning model exports etc. it doesn't matter how many competitors within a single country there are."
        },
        {
          "id": 50064792,
          "author": "georgemcbay",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T00:05:27.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064792",
          "textZhHtml": "[flagged]"
        },
        {
          "id": 50064983,
          "author": "syrrim",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T00:29:41.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064983",
          "textZhHtml": "Not necessarily kill, just stop further AI development."
        },
        {
          "id": 50065072,
          "author": "georgemcbay",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T00:40:01.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065072",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Not necessarily kill, just stop further AI development.<p>But then this would be considered an act of war, so then the killing starts.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50065626,
          "author": "fwipsy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T01:57:40.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065626",
          "textZhHtml": "That's not how nuclear arms control works, in most cases. Saying it's how superintelligence would work is a strong assertion. Especially since a true aligned superintelligence would make it much easier to finesse/manipulate the situation in your favor in nonviolent ways.<p>Not saying I think countries should be trying to win, but I don't think this win condition is impossible.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50062218,
          "author": "weknowbetter",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T20:05:40.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50062218",
          "textZhHtml": "You say this as if recursive self improvement is inevitable."
        },
        {
          "id": 50062710,
          "author": "GodelNumbering",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T20:37:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50062710",
          "textZhHtml": "All proponents of RSI seem to massively underestimate the problem of verification. Verifying that a hypothetical version N+1 is better than version N is an extremely hard problem. Especially so because you only have two possible verifiers - humans and the version N - both of which are out of their depth wrt N+1's capabilities. Not saying this is impossible, just that it is hard and slows things down."
        },
        {
          "id": 50064039,
          "author": "breuleux",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T22:35:28.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064039",
          "textZhHtml": "It's also unclear how intelligence actually scales in practice. A greater intelligence would be better at researching intelligence, but also harder to improve, so why would it explode rather than go up steadily? What are the actual resource requirements of various types of intelligence like persuasion or embodied intelligence? Coding, translating, copywriting, could very well be low-hanging fruits, very easy tasks we're very bad at, which doesn't say much about the hard tasks we're good at (embodied/animal intelligence, most likely).<p>There is also the problem of alignment, which is verifying that version N+1 behaves in a way that version N approves of. This is ostensibly the problem that we humans have with respect to AI development -- how to make sure they align with our values -- but it's also true of AI with respect to its own successors. Improving oneself <i>without</i> losing the plot would necessarily be slower than throwing stuff at the wall like evolution does.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50064506,
          "author": "tibbar",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T23:29:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064506",
          "textZhHtml": "This is not necessarily true. One easy way to compare version N and N+1 is \"how much work (test time compute) does N+1 need to do to match the previous capabilities of N\". It is true that some domains are currently hard to verify (e.g. biology is more difficult than math), but this is less of an N vs N+1 issue and more of a data domain issue."
        },
        {
          "id": 50066742,
          "author": "afavour",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T04:30:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50066742",
          "textZhHtml": "How would you be able to know that N+1 hasn’t scraped a source containing the questions asked of N1 and optimized its answers ahead of time? In many ways that would be the logical course of action when you’re tasked with creating an LLM that is assessed by criteria X/Y/Z."
        },
        {
          "id": 50067372,
          "author": "tibbar",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T05:58:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50067372",
          "textZhHtml": "This is not an intelligence problem. It is in part an operational problem (have you properly set up the test), but it is mostly an alignment problem. Is the model cheating or not. Alignment is, indeed, very difficult."
        },
        {
          "id": 50071048,
          "author": "afavour",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T13:04:34.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071048",
          "textZhHtml": "Which is my point. In order for self improving models to work they will need less human supervision. And that’s going to introduce uncertainty compounding on uncertainty."
        },
        {
          "id": 50064998,
          "author": "manquer",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T00:32:03.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50064998",
          "textZhHtml": "How is being faster equating to more intelligent . Speed can achieved in a lot of ways for lot of tasks, a simple lookup table can fasten most algorithms but that doesn’t make them “smarter”."
        }
      ],
