      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50047373,
          "author": "flowerlad",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T20:16:25.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50047373",
          "textZhHtml": "This will have no impact on Adobe's shenanigans. They offer click-to-cancel, but then charge a cancellation fee equal to what you would have paid for the rest of the one-year term. Their clever justification is that you received a significant discount for committing to a year, so you still owe them for the remaining months if you cancel early. I didn't even realize I had signed up for a one-year commitment. I'm not sure where they buried that clause.<p>I expect more businesses will transition to this strategy to counter NYC's new rule. They won't necessarily lower the price, but you will have to sign up for a one-year term, then they will hit you with a massive cancellation fee if you \"click to cancel\".</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50049228,
          "author": "ceasesurthinko",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T23:32:33.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049228",
          "textZhHtml": "Worth keeping an eye on ArtCraft (<a href=\"https://getartcraft.com/apps\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://getartcraft.com/apps</a>).<p>They're building a free, open-source Adobe Creative Suite alternative entirely in Rust. It runs super fast with minimal overhead, works completely offline, and aims for near 1:1 feature parity with Adobe products like Photoshop, Lightroom, Illustrator, Premiere Pro, and After Effects. It also supports command-based actions, making it easy to integrate into AI agents and automated workflows. They have Microsoft Office alternatives too.</p><p>Their Photoshop clone, PhotoCraft (<a href=\"https://github.com/storytold/photocraft\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/storytold/photocraft</a>), has around 47k GitHub stars right now.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50056650,
          "author": "trollbridge",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:04:25.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50056650",
          "textZhHtml": "Probably not, as projects like this have a tendency to die off once the initial excitement wears off and it becomes hard to find contributors who want to work on an all LLM generated codebase."
        },
        {
          "id": 50065218,
          "author": "jychang",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T01:00:56.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065218",
          "textZhHtml": "You're thinking in past terms, not accounting for future change.<p>Opus 6 or Opus 7 probably would be fine with working on an LLM generated codebase.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50065775,
          "author": "trollbridge",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T02:18:03.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50065775",
          "textZhHtml": "In which case it can just vibe out the entire app for me exactly the way I want it."
        },
        {
          "id": 50070609,
          "author": "bitmasher9",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T12:27:46.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50070609",
          "textZhHtml": "If your career is spent using adobe products you probably want to start with adobe-like as a baseline and make changes on top of it."
        },
        {
          "id": 50055181,
          "author": "Matl",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T12:06:47.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055181",
          "textZhHtml": "Worth keeping an eye for sure, but the vibecoded vibe puts me off."
        },
        {
          "id": 50049610,
          "author": "sodapopcan",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T00:17:32.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049610",
          "textZhHtml": "How does this compare to Affinity Products?  (I know I can ask \"AI\" but I'm asking you, though no pressure to respond)."
        },
        {
          "id": 50049736,
          "author": "spiderice",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T00:31:09.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049736",
          "textZhHtml": "Canva now owns Affinity.  So regardless of the answer to that question, more options are nice.  I have 0 faith that Canva will be good stewards of Affinitys original mission, despite making all of the Affinity products free (for now)"
        },
        {
          "id": 50054595,
          "author": "TeMPOraL",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T11:07:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054595",
          "textZhHtml": "They're already proving not to be good stewards, as Affinity now got compressed into <i>one app</i>, AFAIK it's still lacking 100% feature parity with the original ones, and it being free is just a vehicle to make you sign up for an account, and to continuously show you upsells for the cloud subscription, right there in the UI, with the growing amount of \"pro\" features only available on paid subscription tiers."
        },
        {
          "id": 50057309,
          "author": "InsideOutSanta",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:54:28.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057309",
          "textZhHtml": "I have no doubt that they will eventually enshittify Affinity, but combining the three apps into one was, imo, a good move. The suite was clearly meant to be one app from the start, but was sold as three separate apps for commercial reasons.<p>All three original apps contained each other's functionality. As a result, the new combined app is barely larger than one of the three previous apps, and switching between the modes is now much more convenient and has a better UX.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50059476,
          "author": "sodapopcan",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T17:11:00.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50059476",
          "textZhHtml": "That actually does sound nice, hmmmm."
        },
        {
          "id": 50059466,
          "author": "sodapopcan",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T17:10:12.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50059466",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Canva now owns Affinity.<p><i>Insert Michael Scott \"God no\" meme</i></p><p>That's terrible news.  I only use photo-editing software to lay stuff out for print once in a while, so hopefully I can get away without upgrading indefinitely.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50077367,
          "author": "IAmBroom",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T19:34:54.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50077367",
          "textZhHtml": "So use any of a large group of cheaper, simpler photoediting tools."
        },
        {
          "id": 50057628,
          "author": "stronglikedan",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T15:13:52.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057628",
          "textZhHtml": "Linus Tech Tups tested this and determined that it's nowhere near ready for professional use. It's good enough, or even great, for amateur stuff though, so it has it's place. However, that won't even make a dent in Adobe's books, since the vase majority of their licenses go to professionals."
        },
        {
          "id": 50058744,
          "author": "td521534f12",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T16:26:08.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50058744",
          "textZhHtml": "The LTT video was not about this project. It was some other, significantly more \"vibe coded\" project."
        },
        {
          "id": 50062567,
          "author": "dzonga",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T20:29:05.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50062567",
          "textZhHtml": "there was a company that provides decent alternative to adobe products. I think they were acquired by Canva. used to be one time purchase.<p>not these llm vibe coded shit.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50057298,
          "author": "axus",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T14:53:48.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50057298",
          "textZhHtml": "Isn't the main audience for these applications opposed to AI?  And the businesses that like AI would skip using artists altogether."
        },
        {
          "id": 50052487,
          "author": "robbiep",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T07:13:44.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50052487",
          "textZhHtml": "JFC first release of photocraft was 2 October"
        },
        {
          "id": 50054077,
          "author": "troupo",
          "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T10:07:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50054077",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Worth keeping an eye on ArtCraft<p>No. No it's not.</p><p>Fort all of Adobe's faults, Photoshop and other offerings are <i>products</i>, with support timelines, features added etc. [1] This so far is \"let me vibe-code some thing with no intention of maintaining it further\".</p><p>[1] And there are others competing as well. From opensource like Gimp to Affinity Design, Pixelmator etc.</p>"
        }
      ],
